  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAfter a brief break in showers his afternoon, another round of \nrain will move into the area this evening through tonight as a \nshortwave moves east along a slow-moving frontal boundary. By \nthe time rain moves into the area, the boundary will likely be \nhugging the CWA border or be just to the south, which will make \nthe rainfall forecast somewhat tricky. The heaviest rainfall \nwill likely occur to the south/southwest of the local area, \nalthough there is potential that heavy rain clips the southern \nfringe of the CWA (mainly south of the US-30 corridor). \nAdditional rain accumulation of around an inch (maybe locally\nhigher amounts) is possible in southern zones if the frontal \nboundary ends up being a bit farther north than currently \nexpected. If heavy rainfall materializes in the local area, \nthere may be localized flooding in low-lying and poor drainage \nareas, especially in locations that see prolonged periods of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall. Will need to keep an eye on radar \nand hi-res guidance trends and make adjustments to the forecast\nis needed. Opted to hold off on a Flood Watch due to low \nconfidence in the placement of the heaviest rainfall and the \ngreatest risk of flooding. \n\nRain chances will remain relatively higher through Monday \nmorning with shower activity becoming more isolated Monday\nafternoon and evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nBy Tuesday, Canadian high pressure will shift into the region,\nallowing temperatures to decrease for the majority of the week.\nHighs will be in the 60s Monday through Friday with overnight\nlows in the 50s expected. Friday will likely be in the chilliest\nnight of the week with forecast lows in the 40s at inland\nlocations. While periodic and scattered showers are possible\nthrough Thursday, PoPs will peak on Wednesday as an upper trough\npushes east across the Midwest. Dry weather will return by \nFriday and continue into next weekend.\n\nIt will be quite breezy on Monday and Tuesday due to a tight\npressure gradient over the region as high pressure builds in\nfrom the north and low pressure passes across the Ohio Valley. \nNortheast wind gusts of around 30 mph are expected across NW OH \nwith locally higher gusts to 35 mph possible along the lakeshore\nof NW OH.",
